// chip8: a CHIP-8 emulator in x86-64 assembly (NASM), linked against libc with gcc
// no SDL yet. no display yet. it runs until it hits an opcode it doesn't know.

// build / run
//   make        (needs nasm + gcc)
//   make run    (runs roms/pong.ch8)
//   ./chip8 some.ch8

// CHIP-8 is a fake computer from the 1970s. the whole machine is
//   4096 bytes of memory (ROM is loaded at 0x200)
//   V0..VF, 16 8-bit registers (VF is the flag register)
//   I, a 16-bit address register
//   PC, 16-bit program counter
//   a stack of 16 return addresses
//   64x32 1-bit display, 16 keys, two 60Hz timers
// every instruction is 2 bytes, big endian. x86 is little endian, so rol ax, 8 after the load.

// register map
//   ebx   CHIP-8 PC
//   r12   &state: memory first, then V, I, the stack and its pointer, all at fixed offsets
//         (rip-relative addressing can't take an index, so keep the base in a register)
//   eax   current opcode (handlers read it from here)
//   ecx, edx, esi   scratch

// decode
//   no big switch. upper nibble of the opcode indexes a table of 16 handlers
//     jmp [table + (op >> 12) * 8]
//   handlers jump back to main.next, they don't ret
//   unimplemented opcodes go to op_unknown: it prints "bad opcode OP at PC" to stderr and exits 1
//   every failure goes through die (main.asm): short message on stderr, then exit 1

// exit codes
//   0 clean exit (nothing uses it yet, there is no quit key)
//   1 any failure: no argument, rom can't be opened / is empty / is bigger than 3584 bytes,
//     pc past 0xFFE, stack overflow / underflow, unknown opcode

// done
//   1NNN 2NNN 00EE            jump, call, return
//   3XNN 4XNN 5XY0 9XY0       skips
//   6XNN 7XNN ANNN            set V[X], add to V[X], set I

// todo
//   CXNN, 8XY?, FX07 FX1E FX33 FX55 FX65
//   timers (FX15 FX18) at 60Hz
//   00E0 DXYN EX9E EXA1 FX0A FX29 + font + SDL2 (uncomment the LIBS line in the Makefile)
//   bounds checks on I for the opcodes that read / write memory (DXYN FX33 FX55 FX65)

// this is a learning project. first assembly program. expect bugs.
